WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
ai agents are quietly stripping ads and sluggish menus from smart TVs, turning them fast and simple again. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 200-2. ChatGPT live slides for group work, Kling's video tool was sound, a deep dive on how a helper on your home Wi-Fi maps a TV, then quick bits. You know that feeling when movie night is ready, and the smart TV still tracks what you watch and pushes apps you never wanted? A growing number of people, tech crunch reports, are fighting back by letting

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.780
creating simple artificial intelligence agents take the controls and clean house.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:39.660
Artificial intelligence or AI is software that can handle tasks that you need a person.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.900
An agent is an AI that can take a series of steps on its own, instead of only chatting

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:45.400
back.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:50.800
Picture the TV as a backpack stuffed with flyers, trackers, and extra apps you never packed.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.800
These agents connect to the TV over the same Wi-Fi network your phone already uses.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.920
Think of that Wi-Fi as a shared hallway in your house, where every gadget can knock on

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.180
every other gadget's door.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.400
Once they are in that hallway, the agents explore the TV's menus the way a curious friend

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.620
pokes around a new app.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:13.240
They can even add tricks like pulling videos straight from the internet without extra apps.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:18.700
Almost every teen has sat through a laggy TV during a family stream, so this is not some

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:19.900
far away lab story.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:25.560
It is AI giving regular people more control over devices they already own, instead of waiting

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:26.820
for companies to fix them.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:32.600
If the trend grows, future TVs might ship with fewer forced features, because users now

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.160
have an easy way to remove them.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.440
You can try a version of this right now.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:42.280
Put your computer and your TV on the same Wi-Fi, then ask an AI-like clawed to help reverse

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.040
engineer the TV's controls with one short prompt.

00:01:47.178 --> 00:01:51.638
Reverse Engineer here just means figure out the hidden buttons by watching what the TV does.

00:01:51.638 --> 00:01:56.418
It is like learning a new game by tapping every menu until the secret settings appear.

00:01:57.658 --> 00:02:02.878
Many people report it takes about 5 minutes, and it works on popular brands like Samsung.

00:02:03.678 --> 00:02:08.258
Owners say the set feels noticeably snappier once tracking and ad services are turned off.

00:02:08.838 --> 00:02:13.818
The same method also lets users install apps from outside the official store, something TV

00:02:13.818 --> 00:02:14.958
makers usually block.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:21.130
Changing the password on a home router already teaches the whole trick, even if you have never

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:22.550
thought about TVs this way.

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:27.690
You open a browser on any phone or laptop that is already on that network, and the router

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:29.610
talks back, no special cable.

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:32.770
Everything on the same Wi-Fi can speak to everything else.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:38.190
An AI agent does that same knock on the door move, except it is not changing a password.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:41.690
It quietly walks every menu and setting on the smart TV.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:42.850
Step 1.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.790
It sends a simple request like show me the settings page.

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:46.910
Step 2.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:49.830
It follows the on-screen buttons the way you would with a remote.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:51.030
Step 3.

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:56.210
Because the agent and the TV share the network, it sees the response instantly and keeps going.

00:02:56.710 --> 00:03:00.010
It hunts until it finds the hidden options that control ads or speed.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:05.570
The whole loop feels like a tiny patient helper sitting next to the TV, who never gets board

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:05.990
clicking.

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:10.870
Once the agent learns the layout, it can remember the path and run it again whenever you want

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:11.870
the TV cleaned up.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:17.090
It uses the everyday connection you already have to reach a device that normally only talks

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:18.010
to its own apps.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:23.170
The agent keeps a running list of every screen it visits, so it can jump back without starting

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:23.510
over.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:28.390
That list is like a breadcrumb trail through a maze, or a map you draw while walking

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:29.630
a new Minecraft world.

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:34.810
It also notices when a menu changes after a button press, and it adjusts the next move.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:38.650
That back and forth continues until the agent has mapped the whole system.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:43.630
Then it can carry out the exact sequence of clicks needed to remove bloatware, which is

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:45.790
the extra junk software you never asked for.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:50.650
See it as a patient clicker with a map, and the trick stops feeling like a locked box.

00:03:52.869 --> 00:03:58.489
Open AI added live collaboration to chat GPT slides, so several people can work on the

00:03:58.489 --> 00:03:59.849
same presentation at once.

00:04:00.429 --> 00:04:05.169
Digit reports you type or speak your ideas, and chat GPT turns them into slides.

00:04:05.749 --> 00:04:10.809
Classmates or teammates can jump in, edit text, move pictures, or leave comments while

00:04:10.809 --> 00:04:12.489
everyone watches the changes happen.

00:04:12.489 --> 00:04:17.349
It is like a group chat that is also the slide show, instead of one person doing all the

00:04:17.349 --> 00:04:19.149
work because sharing files was a pain.

00:04:19.789 --> 00:04:24.929
You can click directly on any slide to change text, update charts, or resize images without

00:04:24.929 --> 00:04:25.789
leaving the chat.

00:04:26.209 --> 00:04:31.549
Chat GPT or its helper agent can turn those feedback comments into actual edits automatically.

00:04:32.149 --> 00:04:38.449
To try it, open chat GPT, type create chat GPT slides followed by your topic, then share

00:04:38.449 --> 00:04:39.429
the link with friends.

00:04:39.429 --> 00:04:44.769
Live group editing needs a pro, business, or enterprise plan, which are the paid chat

00:04:44.769 --> 00:04:45.709
GPT plans.

00:04:46.189 --> 00:04:49.889
The free version still lets you build single slides so you can test the idea.

00:04:50.429 --> 00:04:55.189
The finished deck can be presented inside chat GPT, or exported straight to PowerPoint

00:04:55.189 --> 00:04:56.349
or Google Slides.

00:04:59.147 --> 00:05:05.307
The video tool Kling just released version 4.0, and the decoder says it can now make 30 second

00:05:05.307 --> 00:05:06.567
clips with stereo sound.

00:05:07.147 --> 00:05:11.827
stereo sound means the audio has a left and a right side, like headphones, not a flat

00:05:11.827 --> 00:05:12.987
beat from one speaker.

00:05:13.607 --> 00:05:18.267
It can also lip sync in multiple languages, which means the mouth on screen matches the

00:05:18.267 --> 00:05:18.927
words you hear.

00:05:19.527 --> 00:05:24.307
You give it a prompt or a few keyframe images, and it generates smooth video that matches the

00:05:24.307 --> 00:05:24.687
audio.

00:05:25.327 --> 00:05:30.447
Keyframes are still pictures that mark the important poses, like comic panels that the motion has

00:05:30.447 --> 00:05:31.207
to pass through.

00:05:31.207 --> 00:05:36.667
2. That opens quick experiments for short stories, music videos, or meme clips, without

00:05:36.667 --> 00:05:41.947
expensive software. The new version accepts up to 10 keyframe images, and it understands

00:05:41.947 --> 00:05:47.407
prompts up to 8,000 tokens long. Tokens are the little chunks of words the AI reads,

00:05:47.767 --> 00:05:52.687
like Lego bricks that stack into a sentence. Go to the Kling website, sign up for the free

00:05:52.687 --> 00:05:58.087
tier, and try typing a cat dancing on a windowsill with upbeat music. Watch whether the

00:05:58.087 --> 00:06:02.067
motion stays smooth, and whether the music actually feels attached to the picture.

00:06:02.647 --> 00:06:06.827
A new flash version runs lighter if you want faster results on a regular laptop.

00:06:07.447 --> 00:06:11.647
Comparisons with other tools show it keeps pace, but does not pull far ahead.

00:06:12.207 --> 00:06:15.087
The rapid gains in AI video quality may be slowing.

00:06:17.517 --> 00:06:22.377
Tesla is tying employee stock awards to how many Optimus robots come off the factory line.

00:06:22.977 --> 00:06:27.937
Optimus is Tesla's walking robot, shaped like a person, and those extra shares are the

00:06:27.937 --> 00:06:28.257
bonus.

00:06:28.257 --> 00:06:34.377
The same actuators already appear on the new Roadster, controlling arrow surfaces and tank valves.

00:06:35.037 --> 00:06:42.197
Actuators are the little motors that move a joint, like muscles, and arrow surfaces are the flaps that change how air flows over the car.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:52.050
Open AI is improving chat GPT on old programming languages like Coball, a decades-old language still running important systems.

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:58.550
Banks and governments still depend on that kind of code, and the latest models are getting better at reading and updating it

00:06:58.550 --> 00:06:59.850
without breaking anything.

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:05.810
The work focuses on the business logic written in Coball, Fortran, and Visual Basic, meaning

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:10.670
the hidden rules those programs follow, so developers can modernize systems while keeping

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:11.690
what already works.

